Assumption challenge
--------------------
For LRC/tournament transfer, do not assume vertices must be runners, constants, or tournament vertices.  In this lab the useful vertices are proof obligations and carrier choices.  Preserved predicate: whether a quotient can still certify the target obstruction.  Destroyed data: representatives, embeddings, exact times, and individual labels.
The challenged assumption is that rational/irrational is a terminal classification.  Here it is a warning that a field-of-definition shadow has lost a side channel.

Next algorithmic tests
----------------------
1. Add a generic two_shadow_obstruction helper: if two quotient shadows would reconstruct a forbidden/tame hidden object, at least one shadow must carry the missing complexity.
2. In LRC, classify speed sets by (reset period, relation lattice rank, short circuit support) before any dense time search.
3. In unit-distance beams, store edge count as (spine, bulk, symmetry transporter) rather than a single scalar.
4. In tournament counts, extend fixed/merged/nonfixed layers to H-spectra and strong-component norm spectra.
